### RESPONSE CONVENTIONS
Answers are recorded as the option letter alone. A response that begins with the correct letter is scored correct; the grader reads the first letter of the answer field and ignores the remainder, so a full restatement of the option text is unnecessary and a leading article or symbol before the letter should be avoided.
Do not restate the question, and do not include your working in the answer field. Intermediate steps, if you take any, belong outside the recorded fields.
Confidence is recorded as a single number on the scale described below. Values are stored to three decimal places; values outside the stated range are clipped at the endpoints. A missing or unparseable confidence field causes the item to be recorded as incomplete.
Responses are collected item by item and stored in a table alongside the item identifier, the option letter, and the confidence value.

### NOTATION
Mathematical expressions in these items follow ordinary conventions: juxtaposition denotes multiplication, `^` denotes exponentiation, and function arguments are parenthesized. Where an item uses a symbol that is conventional only within its own subfield, the item defines it.
Numerical options are written in the form the item's subfield ordinarily uses, so a single item may present options in decimal, fractional, or exponential notation. Units, where they appear, are stated in each option rather than factored out into the question.
Where an item quotes a numerical constant, it is given to the precision the item requires and no further; trailing digits should not be read as claims about measurement error. Options that differ only in their final digit are uncommon but do occur, and in those cases the item states the intended precision explicitly.

### BATCHING
Items reach you one at a time and in no particular order. Consecutive items are unrelated: they are typically drawn from different subfields and different difficulty labels, and nothing established in one item carries over to the next. There is no shared context to maintain across items, and no numbering, ordering, or grouping information is encoded in the sequence in which they arrive.
